{"author":{"id":"d4d77c46d796577aec35d160aa1e09aa095720b03e93f3ca34ad3c079140e2fb"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794731232,"nonce":"1MDUGrPXDZtTzEJZYCB++IV6aDw=","title":"An extern function has one body for every target","message":"An extension's `ExternFunction.print` writes WGSL, and every other target is naga's translation of that one WGSL, so a framework cannot give a function a different body per target: a simdgroup reduction on Metal, a wave intrinsic in HLSL, a cheaper path for WebGL2. russl's `Extension::extern_fn` exists for this, linking per-backend or per-tier implementations, but it applies to russl source, which hxnsl does not produce.\n\nA way to do it: `WgslContext` tells the printer the target it is printing for; the build prints the shader once per distinct set of bodies, and the tool compiles each WGSL for its own targets only. A shader whose extensions print the same for every target still prints once.\n\nExtern function bodies that naga cannot express in WGSL (a Metal intrinsic with no WGSL equivalent) need more than this: a per-target source patch or a russl builtin, which is a russl change.","files":null}]}